    "material-structuralglass-design": {
      "id": "material-structuralglass-design",
      "content": "",
      "contentPath": "",
      "pageTitle": "",
      "headers": [
        "Attribute (name)",
        "Record",
        "Definition",
        "IFC",
        "IFC Tag",
        "IFC Entity Type",
        "Example Input"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Borosilicate Inclusion",
          "necessary",
          "Borosilicate inclusion refers to the presence of borosilicate compounds or inclusions within the glass, which can influence heat resistance and long-term durability. Recording this long-term allows future assessment of thermal behaviour, compatibility with fire-resistant assemblies, and validity of performance assumptions.",
          "Custom",
          "Pset_MaterialStructuralGlass → Borosilicate",
          "IfcBoolean",
          "TRUE"
        ],
        [
          "Intumescent Interlayer",
          "necessary",
          "Intumescent interlayer indicates whether an interlayer designed to expand under heat (e.g., fire-resistant glazing) is present. Long-term retention is important for verifying fire-performance characteristics, replacement compatibility, and future regulatory compliance.",
          "Custom",
          "Pset_MaterialStructuralGlass → IntumescentLayer",
          "IfcBoolean",
          "FALSE"
        ],
        [
          "Manufacturing Technology",
          "necessary",
          "Manufacturing technology refers to the production method used for the glass, such as tempered, heat-strengthened, annealed, laminated, or chemically strengthened.<br>Keeping this information supports future inspections, repair decisions, and assessment of residual stress patterns that affect breakage behaviour.",
          "Custom",
          "Pset_MaterialStructuralGlass → FabricationProcess",
          "IfcLabel",
          "“Hot-Bending”"
        ],
        [
          "Mass Density",
          "necessary",
          "Mass density is the mass per unit volume of glass, this value varies depending on composition. It can range between 2000 to 8000 kg/m3 Recording this ensures future users can validate weight, lifting requirements, structural assumptions, and load calculations linked to mass.",
          "Existing",
          "Pset_MaterialCommon → MassDensity",
          "IfcMassDensityMeasure<br>(or IfcReal)",
          "2500 \t\t\t[kg/m³]"
        ],
        [
          "Type of Glass",
          "necessary",
          "Type of glass identifies the base category (such as float glass, laminated glass, toughened glass, or fire-rated glass.) This must be retained to understand mechanical behaviour, safety characteristics, and compatibility with future replacements.",
          "Custom",
          "Pset_MaterialStructuralGlass → Type",
          "IfcLabel",
          "“Tempered”"
        ],
        [
          "Type of Interlayer",
          "necessary",
          "Type of interlayer refers to the material used between glass layers (e.g., PVB, EVA, SGP, intumescent gel). Long-term recording ensures that future maintenance or replacements use compatible materials to maintain structural, acoustic, and safety performance.",
          "Custom",
          "Pset_MaterialStructuralGlass → Interlayer",
          "IfcLabel",
          "“PVB”"
        ],
        [
          "Bending Strength",
          "necessary",
          "The bending strength (also called flexural strength) of glass is the maximum stress it can withstand before failure when subjected to bending loads. It is highly dependent on surface condition, edge quality, and heat treatment (e.g., annealed vs. tempered). This property is critical for panels spanning between supports or subjected to wind and impact loads.",
          "Existing",
          "Pset_MechanicalPanelInPlane → BendingStrength",
          "IfcPressureMeasure<br>(or IfcReal)",
          "120 \t\t\t[MPa]"
        ],
        [
          "Poisson Ratio",
          "optional",
          "Poisson’s ratio is the ratio of lateral strain to longitudinal strain when glass is subjected to uniaxial stress. For most glass types, this value is approximately 0.22–0.24. It influences deformation compatibility in composite assemblies and affects stress distribution under load.",
          "Existing",
          "Pset_MaterialMechanical → PoissonRatio",
          "IfcPositiveRatioMeasure<br>(or IfcReal)",
          "0.22"
        ],
        [
          "Shear Modulus",
          "optional",
          "Shear modulus (also called modulus of rigidity) quantifies the material’s resistance to shear deformation. For glass, it is derived from Young’s modulus and Poisson’s ratio and is essential for analyzing laminated glass performance, particularly in composite action between layers under load.",
          "Existing",
          "Pset_MaterialMechanical → ShearModulus",
          "IfcModulusOfElasticityMeasure<br>(or IfcReal)",
          "30,000 \t\t\t[MPa]"
        ],
        [
          "Tensile Strength",
          "optional",
          "Tensile strength is the maximum tensile stress glass can sustain before fracture. Glass is inherently brittle, and its tensile strength is strongly influenced by surface flaws and edge finishing. Heat-treated glass (tempered) can exhibit tensile strength up to 120 MPa, while annealed glass is typically around 40 MPa.",
          "Existing",
          "Pset_MechanicalPanelInPlane → TensileStrength",
          "IfcModulusOfElasticityMeasure<br>(or IfcReal)",
          "45 \t\t\t[MPa]"
        ],
        [
          "Thermal Expansion Coefficient",
          "optional",
          "The thermal expansion coefficient defines the rate at which glass expands or contracts with temperature changes, typically around 8–9 × 10⁻⁶ /°C for soda-lime glass. This property is crucial for detailing connections and predicting thermal stresses in large façades or assemblies exposed to temperature gradients.",
          "Existing",
          "Pset_MaterialMechanical → ThermalExpansionCoefficient",
          "IfcThermalExpansionCoefficientMeasure<br>(or IfcReal)",
          "9×10⁻⁶ \t\t\t[1/K]"
        ],
        [
          "Youngs Modulus",
          "optional",
          "Young’s modulus (elastic modulus) measures the stiffness of glass in tension or compression, typically 70–74 GPa for soda-lime glass. It governs deflection under load and is fundamental for structural analysis, especially when assessing serviceability limits.",
          "Existing",
          "Pset_MaterialMechanical → YoungModulus",
          "IfcModulusOfElasticityMeasure<br>(or IfcReal)",
          "70,000 \t\t\t[MPa]"
        ]
      ],
      "note": ""
    },
